  "problem": "",
  "worked_solution": "A dilation with center at the origin and a factor of $k$ can be represented by the function\n\n$$\n(x,y)↦(kx,ky)\\,.\n$$\n\nApplying this function to the point $P$ and equating the result to ${P}^{′},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(k\\cdot (-4),k\\cdot (-2)=(3,1.5)\\,⟹\\,\\{\\begin{aligned}-4k=3 \\\\ -2k=1.5.\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $k=\\frac{3}{-4}=\\frac{1.5}{-2}=-\\frac{3}{4}.$   \n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14221848/assets/d3b818151bcfddd3fd4f642463ba4e06ba1844d4b772652dadde0d3049e441ee.png)",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
